WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.620
An actor with style and delivery can elevate
the form — and the human spirit — with

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.940
their performance, but at the end of the day,
it's also a profession.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.620
Although a role in a critically acclaimed
indie flick might earn a few pats on the back

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.820
and awards, at the end of the day, it's usually
blockbusters that pay the bills.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.560
These actors have been guilty of taking gigs
strictly for the money — but to their credit,

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.680
they were totally upfront about it afterward.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.420
Sir Michael Caine in Jaws: The Revenge

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.020
Sir Michael Caine is one of the most acclaimed
actors in modern cinema.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.420
His six-decade filmography is laced with award-winning
performances, including his roles in Woody

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:40.160
Allen's Hannah and Her Sisters, as well as
several nominations for Alfie and The Quiet

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:40.680
American.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.340
Naturally, not every performance can be a
winner and not every film is destined to be

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.660
met with critical acclaim — and like the
rest of us, Mr. Caine still needs to eat.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.780
Hence the actor's participation in fare such
as Jaws: The Revenge, the fourth and final

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.500
movie in the Jaws franchise.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.920
The knighted thespian is unapologetic for
his presence in the movie — which scored

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:06.100
an embarrassing 14 percent audience review
on Rotten Tomatoes — and his famously candid

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.900
quote sums things up perfectly.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.020
"I have never seen it, but by all accounts
it is terrible.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.380
However, I have seen the house that it built,
and it is terrific."

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.380
Sir Alec Guinness in Star Wars: A New Hope

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.000
For the younger members of the cast, 1977's
original Star Wars: A New Hope was a springboard

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.140
to a film career.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.480
But for their veteran counterparts, the space
opera was just another job.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.860
Take Alec Guinness, for example.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.020
Already the Academy Award-winning star of
renowned films like Doctor Zhivago and Lawrence

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.540
of Arabia, Guinness — who plays Jedi Knight
Obi-Wan Kenobi in the original trilogy — was

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.140
reportedly nonplussed with his newfound Star
Wars stardom.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.740
His autobiography includes his recollection
of a moment when he discouraged an overzealous

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.689
fan from watching the films again, and he
clearly saw the part as a paycheck in numerous

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:53.280
interviews.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.320
"And then when I opened it and found it was
science fiction I thought, 'Oh crumbs, this

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.380
is simply not for me.'"

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.620
While on set he also wrote to longtime friend
Anne Kauffman, "I just think, thankfully,

00:02:04.639 --> 00:02:08.190
of the lovely bread, which will help me keep
going until next April."

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.060
The force was strong with his wallet.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.100
Jackie Chan in Rush Hour

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.951
Since Jackie Chan's "official" Western audience
debut in 1995's Rumble in the Bronx, Chan

00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:22.470
has remained a consistent presence on the
American cultural landscape, even enjoying

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.470
a stint as an animated character in Jackie
Chan Adventures.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:30.260
In the late '90s, though, seeking to further
his global influence and crossover appeal,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.360
Chan teamed up with Chris Tucker for the Brett
Ratner-directed action-comedy flick Rush Hour.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.631
It proved an undeniable box office darling,
spinning off several sequels, but later, Chan

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.260
admitted his own distaste for the goofball
movie.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.400
"I have reasons to do each film, I have something
to say.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.120
Unlike Rush Hour—there was no reason [in
making it], you just give me the money and

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:50.620
I'm fine.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.580
I dislike Rush Hour the most, but ironically
it sold really well in the U.S. and Europe."

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.500
"Do you understand the words that are coming
out from my mouth?

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.260
"Nobody understands the words coming out of
your mouth, man!"

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.860
Well, ain't that a kick in the chops.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.500
Glenn Close in Guardians of the Galaxy

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.320
Glenn Close's talent speaks for itself, but
in today's film industry, the bulk of the

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.540
films in development are would-be tentpoles,
and prestige pictures are few and far between.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:20.100
It's left actors like Close, who appeared
in Marvel's first Guardians of the Galaxy

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.530
movie, to subsidize their art through sometimes
surprising means.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.900
"I just did two independent films this summer
and I had an absolute ball with fantastic

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.360
actors, but in August, I'm gonna go off and
do the next generation of Marvel Comics/Disney

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.520
and I get to be the chief police of the galaxy.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.120
I'm doing that because it will then afford
me to go do the other kind of movies that

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:39.820
I really love."

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.180
Sadly, under the circumstances, her mercenary
attitude makes sense.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.680
So moviegoers can certainly forgive Ms. Close
for her fiscally based rationale.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.100
Kareem Abdul-Jabbar in Airplane

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.200
Whatever happened to Kareem Abdul-Jabbar's
movie career?

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.730
Sure, he wasn't about to win any Oscars, but
he was pretty solid for a basketball star

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.560
turned actor.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:04.580
His Airplane! role, as the copilot Roger Murdock
marked the beginning of a post-NBA acting

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.860
and voicework career.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:07.480
"We have clearance, Clarence"

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.180
"Roger Roger.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.040
What's our vector, Victor?"

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.920
Abdul-Jabbar's motives to branch out into
acting may not have been artistically pure,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.780
but he did have a natural charisma that, in
addition to his colossal height, made him

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:18.660
stand out.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.840
Airplane! co-directors David and Jerry Zucker
said they offered Abdul-Jabbar a cool sum

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.420
of $30,000 for the role — and the hoopster's
manager got back to them with a very "creative"

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.020
reason for a slight hike in rate.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.240
"The agent asked for $35,000 because that's
how much this rug cost that Kareem wanted

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:36.740
to buy."

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.520
"...our initial reaction was, 'That's got
to be the best line we've ever heard from

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:41.020
an agent.'"

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.280
"But then a couple of weeks later, there's
an article in Time with a picture of Kareem

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.520
standing in front of the oriental rug that
he'd bought for $35,000 after we'd paid him."

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.180
At least Abdul-Jabbar was up-front about his
reel requirements.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.820
Betsy Palmer in Friday the 13th

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.020
One of the big gotcha moments in the horror
blockbuster Friday the 13th is the climactic

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.820
reveal that Jason isn't the murderer, but
rather, it's his mother played by Betsy Palmer.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.000
For the killer role, director Sean Cunningham
recruited TV vet Betsy Palmer, whose credits

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.820
include Studio One in Hollywood and Love American
Style.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:20.080
Apparently, the opportunity came at the perfect
time, as Palmer told MovieWeb.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.500
"The phone rang and my agent said, 'How would
you like to do a movie?' and I said, 'Great!

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.430
That will pay for the car I want to buy.'

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.500
And I said, 'Oh… nobody is ever going to
see this.'"

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.780
Boy, that sure backfired on her — the movie's
visibility, that is.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:35.740
Hopefully not the car.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:36.820
Thanks for watching!

